{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And the children of Israel groaned from the servitude, and they cried out.</b> It is somewhat difficult: why did the verse not combine them and state, 'And the children of Israel groaned and cried out from the servitude'? It would appear that the groaning was in the heart, and it was on account of the servitude. However, as for the outcry, Israel did not attribute it to the servitude, for they thought themselves worthy of being redeemed on account of their deeds, even had this harsh servitude not existed. But in the eyes of HaShem, blessed be He, they were wicked and sinful and unworthy of being redeemed except on account of the servitude, as it is written (Zechariah 1:15), ״כִּי קֶצֶף גָּדוֹל אֲנִי קֹצֵף עַל הַגּוֹיִם אֲשֶׁר קָצַפְתִּי מְעָט וְהֵמָּה עָזְרוּ לְרָעָה״. Therefore it says, ״וַתַּעַל שַׁוְעָתָם אֶל הָאֱלֹהִים מִן הָעֲבֹדָה״ — for they helped for evil, as said." "And one may explain further, that there were two factions in Israel: the better ones among them cried out and prayed to Elohim to save them from the heaviness of the servitude, but the lesser ones among them did not pray to HaShem; they only cried out like those who pick a quarrel against HaShem. And concerning them it says, ״וַיִּזְעָקוּ״ (Exodus 2:23), and therefore it did not include them together. For what is stated, ״וַיֵּאָנְחוּ בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל מִן הָעֲבֹדָה״ (Exodus 2:23), refers to the good ones who prayed to HaShem, for the essence of prayer is in the heart; and ״וַיִּזְעָקוּ״ speaks of the lesser ones who cried out like those who pick a quarrel. Therefore it says, ״וַתַּעַל שַׁוְעָתָם אֶל הָאֱלֹהִים״ (Exodus 2:23) — from the factions that cried out to Elohim on account of the servitude, an outcry ascended, but not from the faction of those who cried out without prayer." "And some say, that this outcry was in the open: they said before the Egyptians that it was on account of the death of the king, as mourners over him, but the groaning was in the heart, on account of the servitude. For out of fear they were not permitted to say before the Egyptians that they were crying out on account of the servitude, and therefore the verse did not combine them."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "The Aramaic idiom לְעָרְבִינְהוּ וְלִתְנִינְהוּ (lit. 'to mix them and to give them') is rendered as 'combine them and state them together.'", 1 "קוֹרְאֵי תִּגָּר rendered literally as 'those who pick a quarrel,' preserving the confrontational force of the idiom."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:18:04.647093852Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}